{"id":"sc900_p2_q001","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"security_compliance_concepts","subobjective":"encryption and hashing","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A security team needs to protect a message so an authorized recipient can later recover the original plaintext. Which operation best fits this requirement?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"Hashing"},{"id":"a","text":"Encryption"},{"id":"c","text":"Authorization"},{"id":"d","text":"Federation"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Encryption transforms readable data so it can be recovered with the appropriate decryption key. Hashing is designed as a one-way operation rather than a reversible confidentiality mechanism.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"tags":["encryption and hashing","encryption_and_hashing"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"encryption_and_hashing","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-security_compliance_concepts","semantic_family_id":"encryption_vs_hashing","stem_style":"distinction_comparison","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q002","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"security_compliance_concepts","subobjective":"governance risk and compliance","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A company identifies the possibility that a supplier outage could interrupt a critical business service and evaluates its likelihood and impact. Which GRC concept is most directly involved?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Governance"},{"id":"c","text":"Authentication"},{"id":"b","text":"Risk management"},{"id":"d","text":"Network segmentation"}],"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Risk management includes identifying and evaluating potential events that could affect objectives, including their likelihood and impact.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"tags":["grc concepts","grc_concepts"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"grc_concepts","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-security_compliance_concepts","semantic_family_id":"grc_risk_identification","stem_style":"short_scenario","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q003","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"security_compliance_concepts","subobjective":"shared responsibility model","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A company runs its own operating system inside an Azure virtual machine. Under the shared responsibility model, who is responsible for patching that guest operating system?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Microsoft only"},{"id":"b","text":"The customer"},{"id":"c","text":"The internet service provider"},{"id":"d","text":"The hardware manufacturer"}],"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"For an infrastructure-as-a-service virtual machine, the customer manages the guest operating system, including its configuration and patching, while the cloud provider manages the underlying physical infrastructure.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"tags":["shared responsibility model","shared_responsibility_model"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"shared_responsibility_model","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-security-concepts-methodologies/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-security_compliance_concepts","semantic_family_id":"shared_responsibility_model","stem_style":"short_scenario","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q004","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"identity_concepts","subobjective":"identity as the primary security perimeter","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Why is identity commonly described as a primary security perimeter in modern cloud environments?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"Cloud services no longer require authorization checks"},{"id":"c","text":"Identity replaces the need to protect data"},{"id":"d","text":"Only on-premises users have identities"},{"id":"a","text":"Users can access cloud resources from many networks and locations, so access decisions must strongly protect identities"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Cloud access is not limited to one trusted network boundary. Protecting identities and controlling their access is therefore central to securing modern resources.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"tags":["identity primary security perimeter","identity_primary_security_perimeter"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"identity_primary_security_perimeter","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-identity_concepts","semantic_family_id":"identity_primary_security_perimeter","stem_style":"direct_concept","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q005","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"identity_concepts","subobjective":"identity providers","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"What is the primary role of an identity provider in an authentication flow?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Store application encryption keys"},{"id":"c","text":"Filter network packets between subnets"},{"id":"b","text":"Verify identity and issue or supply identity information used by relying services"},{"id":"d","text":"Classify documents for retention"}],"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"An identity provider authenticates identities and provides identity information or tokens that applications and services can rely on.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"tags":["identity providers","identity_providers"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"identity_providers","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-identity_concepts","semantic_family_id":"identity_providers","stem_style":"direct_concept","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q006","exam":"SC-900","domain":"security_compliance_identity","objective":"identity_concepts","subobjective":"directory services and Active Directory","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Which capability is characteristic of a directory service such as Active Directory?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"Inspecting HTTP requests for web attacks"},{"id":"a","text":"Maintaining organized information about identities and resources so they can be located and managed"},{"id":"c","text":"Calculating a compliance score"},{"id":"d","text":"Orchestrating security incident playbooks"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Directory services organize and expose information about identities and resources so administrators and applications can locate and manage them.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"tags":["directory services active directory","directory_services_active_directory"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"directory_services_active_directory","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-identity-principles-concepts/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-identity_concepts","semantic_family_id":"directory_services_active_directory","stem_style":"capability_selection","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q007","exam":"SC-900","domain":"microsoft_entra","objective":"entra_identity_types_and_function","subobjective":"Microsoft Entra ID overview","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"An organization wants one cloud identity service to authenticate users to Microsoft 365 and other integrated applications. Which service is designed for this role?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"Microsoft Sentinel"},{"id":"c","text":"Microsoft Purview Audit"},{"id":"d","text":"Azure DDoS Protection"},{"id":"a","text":"Microsoft Entra ID"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Microsoft Entra ID is Microsoft's cloud identity and access management service and can provide authentication and access for Microsoft and integrated applications.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"tags":["entra id overview","entra_id_overview"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"entra_id_overview","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-entra_identity_types_and_function","semantic_family_id":"entra_id_overview","stem_style":"capability_selection","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q008","exam":"SC-900","domain":"microsoft_entra","objective":"entra_identity_types_and_function","subobjective":"identity types including agent ID","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"An automated application needs to authenticate to another service without a person signing in interactively. Which identity category best represents this non-human application identity?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Workload identity"},{"id":"b","text":"Guest user identity"},{"id":"c","text":"Consumer identity only"},{"id":"d","text":"Retention identity"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Workload identities represent software workloads such as applications or services that need to authenticate and access resources without an interactive human user.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"tags":["identity types including agent id","identity_types_including_agent_id"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"identity_types_including_agent_id","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-entra_identity_types_and_function","semantic_family_id":"entra_workload_identity","stem_style":"short_scenario","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q009","exam":"SC-900","domain":"microsoft_entra","objective":"entra_identity_types_and_function","subobjective":"hybrid identity","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"What is the defining goal of a hybrid identity approach?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"Keep every cloud identity completely unrelated to on-premises directories"},{"id":"c","text":"Replace authentication with network filtering"},{"id":"a","text":"Use related identity information across on-premises and cloud environments"},{"id":"d","text":"Store secrets in a SIEM"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Hybrid identity connects on-premises and cloud identity environments so organizations can provide a consistent identity experience across them.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"tags":["hybrid identity","hybrid_identity"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"hybrid_identity","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-basic-services-identity-types/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-entra_identity_types_and_function","semantic_family_id":"hybrid_identity","stem_style":"direct_concept","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
{"id":"sc900_p2_q010","exam":"SC-900","domain":"microsoft_entra","objective":"entra_authentication","subobjective":"authentication methods","difficulty":"beginner","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Which option is an example of an authentication method used to prove a user's identity rather than an authorization rule?","choices":[{"id":"b","text":"A Microsoft Entra directory role"},{"id":"a","text":"Windows Hello for Business"},{"id":"c","text":"A retention label"},{"id":"d","text":"A network security group"}],"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Windows Hello for Business is an authentication method. Directory roles and network or retention controls determine permissions or policy rather than serving as the user's authentication method.","references":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-authentication-capabilities/"],"tags":["authentication methods","authentication_methods"],"source":{"title":"Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-authentication-capabilities/"},"metadata":{"origin":"manual","batch":"phase2-batch-01","blueprint_leaf_id":"authentication_methods","source_authority":"microsoft_learn","source_urls":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/explore-authentication-capabilities/"],"source_retrieved_at":"2026-09-11","source_family_id":"learn-entra_authentication","semantic_family_id":"authentication_methods","stem_style":"distinction_comparison","future_probe_suitability":"eligible","authoring_origin":"original_from_official_source"}}
